  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(3)+{f}^{\u2032}(3)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(3,5),$ so we have $f(3)=5.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{\u2032}(3)=2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =5+2(x-3) \\\\  & =5+2x-6 \\\\  & =2x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(2.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=2.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.6) & \u2248L(2.6) \\\\  & =2(2.6)-1 \\\\  & =4.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(2,5),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=3,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{overestimate}.$",
